  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal warmth through Friday with some\nrain across the region.\n\nA large upper trough over the western Great Lakes will steadily move \nnorth and east into southern Ontario through the end of this week. A \nleading shortwave ahead of it is set to move across the central \nAlleghenies and Mid-Atlantic tonight into Thursday. This has\nbegun to produce scattered light showers, generally from the\nShenandoah Valley westward. Low-level profiles have begun to\nsaturate so some of this likely has begun to reach the ground. A\nfew tenths of an inch of rain are forecast along and west of \nthe Blue Ridge, which won't be much but still something after \nover two weeks of no rain. Those east of the Blue Ridge likely \nsee less rain due to lingering low- level dry air and loss of \nupper level support as the shortwave quickly moves east of the \narea. Aside from a few showers in the afternoon, most locations \nare going to be dry through Thursday evening. \n\nConditions look favorable for a band of heavier rain to redevelop \njust east of I-95 Thursday night into Friday. This is due to the \ninteraction of the surface high over the western Atlantic and the \napproaching strong upper trough from the OH Valley. A narrow band of \nfrontogenesis likely develops where low-level convergence is \nmaximized near the right-entrance region of a strengthening jet \nstreak, and aided by modest moisture advection. The current forecast \nhas around 1-1.25\" of rain over southern Maryland, though some of \nthe higher end ensemble guidance indicate upwards of 2\" is possible. \nNot expecting any flood concerns due to the location of where this \nis going to occur and lack of precip in recent weeks. There is going \nto be a sharp cutoff in rain amounts to the west of the main band \nwhere rain is light and passive. \n\nAs the upper trough swings through the region Friday night, the main \nmid-level vorticity associated with it cuts off and that takes about \nanother 24-36 hours to move east over the Atlantic. While conditions \ndry out for most of us by Friday evening, there will be upslope rain \nshowers in the Alleghenies through Saturday morning. \n\nAbove normal temperatures continue through Friday with highs reach \nthe 70s each day. Cloud cover and some cold advection Friday keep \nhighs a bit cooler, but only in the upper 60s to around 70F. A \ncolder airmass finally sweeps in behind the FROPA Friday night as \nlows drop to the upper 30s to upper 40s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler, but seasonable temperatures and Appalachian \nrain showers expected this weekend as an upper disturbance moves to \nthe north and adjacent to the region.\n\nAn upper level disturbance will follow a low pressure system to the \nnortheast and east of the region Saturday into Sunday. This \ndisturbance will bring cloud cover to the western and northern areas \nas well as rain showers in the Appalachians Saturday. Otherwise, dry \nconditions with a gusty breeze is anticipated throughout the weekend \nand into the early part of next week. There should be less wind \nTuesday. Temperatures will be cooler than recent days but should be \nclose to seasonable Saturday through Tuesday. Highs should be in the \nlower to middle 60s with cooler temperatures in the mountains \nSaturday through Tuesday. Lows mid 30s to lower 40s each night.",
